#!/bin/sh
# Load the BCM53125 managed-switch driver (the `switch` feature, now built =m to
# keep the EA-3 bzImage inside CEFDK's bootlinux window — see
# board/ea/common/features/switch/linux.fragment).
#
# WHY an init script and not modules-load.d: this image has no systemd and no
# modules-load.d reader, so modules are loaded by modprobe from init, the same
# way S41wifi-ap loads the radio. Features have no rootfs-overlay hook of their
# own, so the switch autoload lives in the EA family overlay; it is a NO-OP on
# EA boards built without the switch feature (b53_spi.ko simply is not in the
# image, so modprobe fails harmlessly).
#
# WHY before S40net (S39 < S40): once the b53 driver binds, the SoC MAC becomes
# the switch's DSA CPU port and the real jacks appear as lan1/lan2. S40net brings
# those up (OHC_DSA_PORTS) and, if DSA has NOT attached, falls back to the master
# — so loading here gives DSA the chance to attach first. modprobe b53_spi pulls
# b53, dsa_core and the Broadcom tag module via modules.dep.
#
# The board glue (spi-ea-b53-board.c) is built in and already registered the
# spi:bcm53125 device at subsys_initcall; loading the module now binds it by
# deferred probe. We wait briefly for the bind so S40net sees the slave netdevs.

MOD=b53_spi
DRV=/sys/bus/spi/drivers/b53-switch

start() {
    # bridge + 8021q are modules now (built in they pushed the 2026.02 bzImage over
    # CEFDK's window — see features/switch/linux.fragment). DSA wants bridging
    # present, so load them first; harmless if absent.
    modprobe bridge >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    modprobe 8021q  >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    # modprobe fails harmlessly on an EA board built without the switch feature
    # (b53_spi.ko is simply not in the image) — same guard style as S41wifi-ap.
    modprobe "$MOD" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
        echo "ea-switch: $MOD not in this image, skipping"
        return 0
    }
    # Best-effort wait for the driver to bind the spi:bcm53125 device, so the
    # lan1/lan2 DSA slaves exist by the time S40net runs. A bound device shows as
    # an spiN.N symlink in the driver dir. S40net's fallback keeps the board
    # reachable on the primary jack even if this times out.
    i=0
    while [ $i -lt 10 ]; do
        for e in "$DRV"/spi*; do
            [ -e "$e" ] && { echo "ea-switch: $MOD bound $(basename "$e")"; return 0; }
        done
        sleep 1; i=$((i + 1))
    done
    echo "ea-switch: $MOD loaded (not bound yet; S40net falls back if needed)"
}

# Build the configured switch topology (default: both DSA ports bridged into
# br-lan, STP on) now that the driver has bound and lan1/lan2 exist — BEFORE
# S40net (S40) leases, so the bridge is the interface that gets the address.
# switchd apply is idempotent, best-effort, and writes the L3 handoff
# (/run/ohc/net.conf) that S40net reads. A no-op where switchd is not shipped
# (boards without the `switch` feature) or OHC_DSA_PORTS is empty.
apply_topology() {
    [ -x /opt/ohc/bin/switchd ] || return 0
    [ -r /opt/ohc/board.env ] && . /opt/ohc/board.env
    export OHC_DSA_PORTS
    [ -n "$OHC_DSA_PORTS" ] || return 0
    /opt/ohc/bin/switchd apply
}

stop() {
    modprobe -r "$MOD" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

case "$1" in
    start) start; apply_topology ;;
    stop) stop ;;
    restart) stop; start ;;
    *) echo "usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac
